Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: mt7530: keep ports with VLAN subinterfaces out of
 the hardware bridge

Tomato64 does not use a VLAN-aware bridge. A tagged VLAN on a port is an
802.1Q subinterface (ethX.VID) that is a member of another bridge, while
the port itself may be an untagged member of the LAN bridge.

A VLAN-unaware hardware bridge is transparent to VLAN tags (the driver
sets MT7530_VLAN_TRANSPARENT on such ports), so the switch forwards
tagged frames between its bridged ports without the kernel's VLAN device
seeing them. Measured on the MT7988 built-in switch of a GL-BE14000, with
one port untagged in br0 and also carrying VLAN 2 for another bridge: a
host on a different, plain br0 port got ARP replies from the VLAN 2 host
by tagging its own frames, and 40 tagged broadcasts sent into the plain
port all left the VLAN port (switch MIB counters), none of them sent by
software.

Refuse the hardware bridge for a port that has VLAN subinterfaces. DSA
then bridges that port in software and the remaining ports keep hardware
forwarding. Unlike on the YT9224 this can be done per port: a standalone
mt7530 port forwards only to the CPU port and uses FID_STANDALONE, so its
lookups never hit addresses learned on the bridged ports.

Tomato64 creates VLAN devices before building bridges (start_vlan() then
start_lan()) and removes them after, so the check at join time suffices.
VLAN-aware bridges are not affected.

The module parameter hw_bridge_vlan_ports lifts the restriction for
testing (/sys/module/mt7530/parameters/hw_bridge_vlan_ports); it applies
the next time a port joins a bridge.

Tomato64-specific.
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/mt7530.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/mt7530.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  */
 #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
 #include <linux/if_bridge.h>
+#include <linux/if_vlan.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/mdio.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
@@ -1390,6 +1391,32 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Tomato64 puts a tagged VLAN on a port as an 802.1Q subinterface (ethX.VID)
+ * that is a member of another bridge, while the port itself may be an untagged
+ * member of the LAN bridge. A VLAN-unaware hardware bridge is transparent to
+ * VLAN tags: the switch floods such a port's tagged broadcasts to the other
+ * bridged ports and forwards tagged frames between them by MAC address, so a
+ * host on any bridged port can reach a VLAN carried on another port just by
+ * tagging its own frames. The kernel's VLAN device never sees that traffic.
+ *
+ * Keep a port that carries VLAN subinterfaces out of the hardware bridge. DSA
+ * then bridges that one port in software. The other ports keep hardware
+ * forwarding: a standalone port only talks to the CPU port and uses its own
+ * FID (FID_STANDALONE), so its lookups never hit addresses learned on the
+ * bridged ports.
+ *
+ * Tomato64 creates the subinterfaces before it builds bridges and removes
+ * them after, so checking at join time is enough. A VLAN-aware bridge is left
+ * alone: there the switch enforces VLAN membership itself.
+ *
+ * /sys/module/mt7530/parameters/hw_bridge_vlan_ports = Y lifts this for
+ * testing; it takes effect the next time a port joins a bridge.
+ */
+static bool mt7530_hw_bridge_vlan_ports;
+module_param_named(hw_bridge_vlan_ports, mt7530_hw_bridge_vlan_ports, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(hw_bridge_vlan_ports,
+		 "Allow hardware bridging on ports that carry VLAN subinterfaces");
+
 static int
 mt7530_port_bridge_join(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
 			struct dsa_bridge bridge, bool *tx_fwd_offload,
@@ -1397,6 +1424,16 @@
 {
 	struct mt7530_priv *priv = ds->priv;
 
+	if (!mt7530_hw_bridge_vlan_ports && !br_vlan_enabled(bridge.dev) &&
+	    vlan_uses_dev(dsa_to_port(ds, port)->user)) {
+		dev_info(priv->dev,
+			 "port %d carries VLAN subinterfaces, bridging it in software\n",
+			 port);
+		NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack,
+				   "Port carries VLAN subinterfaces");
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
+
 	mutex_lock(&priv->reg_mutex);
 
 	mt7530_update_port_member(priv, port, bridge.dev, true);
